How Does a PCB Layout Affect Your Return Path and Signal Integrity?
The answer to this question really depends on the frequency content of signals in your board, as well as the trace/component arrangement and your layer stack. Modern PCBs are generally routed over a ground plane, and it is generally assumed that the return current is always induced directly below a signal thanks to the substrate’s parasitic capacitance.
In these images, the return current path in the ground plane clearly depends on the signal’s frequency. In the DC case (not shown), the current takes the shortest path back to the return point (just a straight line), which is just the path of least resistance.
