Reflow soldering is the most popular and widely used method of connecting surface mount technology (SMT) components to aprinted circuit board (PCB). The process is focusing on creating acceptable solder joints by pre-heated components, which provides the opportunity to melt the solder without causing damage by overheating.
Preheat- During this phase of the process the printed circuit board, components and solder are heated to a specific dwell or soak temperature, with the main aspect, which is not to be heated too quickly (usually no more than +2 degrees a second). Fast heating could cause component defects such as solder paste slattering or cracks.
The time in which the solder is held above the melting point temperature (time above liquidus) is important to ensure that the “wetting” occurs between the printed board and the components. The time is about 30 to 60 seconds and it should not exceed to avoid the creation of brittle solder joints.
Make sure to never cool down the assembly too rapidly as it could lead to malfunctions. The recommendation here is to keep the rate of cooling above 3 degrees a second.
The PCB industry has many different reflowing components requiring certain temperatures.
Advanced printed circuit board assemblers like FUMAX use a reflow soldering oven with 10 temperature zones.
Here are the essentials that lead to an efficient reflow soldering:
Poor quality of PCB, solder paste, and components will lead to undesirable results. If the printed circuit boards were not stored as per the quality requirements, it often results in poor solder ability during the reflow process.
