Use the PREDICT Model To Ease Anxiety About Future
We are in the thick of the Covid-19 pandemic and everyone is anxious, everyone is feeling blindsided by the impact on the global economy.
If ever there was a time to ’stay calm’ and ‘carry on’ this is it.
In times of intense uncertainty, we are being called upon individually to be more flexible, to shift the mindset and to see the potential positives.
We use the PREDICT model with our clients and in this newsletter, I would like to share how to apply it either personally or organizationally when dealing with uncertainty.
What are the patterns of the events that are happening right now?
What are the challenges with what is happening right now and how do they relate to past challenges?
When similar events to those happening right now happened in the past what changes did it force?
How have the patterns of ‘change’ in the past forced change and that are happening now forcing changes?
What are the changes that we are most afraid of right now?
(Business impact/personal job/work impact)
What are the potential positives of this current uncertainty?
How can we leverage the forced changes on ourselves or on the business as an opportunity?
What should we start doing right now?
Mine the data that you receive from the brainstorm/sharing and seek the patterns.
Based on a visual dashboard choose what resonates as potential actions for you/your company/your culture.
Build a daily action plan for self/team/company based on the priority list.
Based on all of the previous steps what stands out clearly as what needs to change NOW?
For example, if your company does not have a remote work plan in place the Covid-19 virus has forced the need for such a plan.
What is preventing me/us from seeing the current uncertainty as an opportunity for creativity and elevated thinking?
If we look ahead at the next month, 2 months, 3 months what could the actions we take today do to create a new future?
How we respond during times of challenge determines how we flex and remain agile through change.
Emotionally we can self resource through meditation, deep breathing, outdoor walking, listening to music, turning off the media for a while, talking with others going through the same thing – these things we CAN control.
