The term “soft skills” implies weak or non-essential and so I say, ‘No more soft skills!’.
I have been on this mission for over a decade!
The military set out to distinguish the difference between working ‘hard’ and the skills used to inspire and engage the teams. Paul G. Whitmore and his team came up with the distinctions between working with something that is physically hard like a machine and compared to that which is soft to the touch.
And so, the term ‘soft skills’ became the term applied to skills that were more intangible to measure such as communication skills, conflict resolution skills, understanding personality styles, and then later onGoleman’s work on emotional intelligence.
The future of work requires a ‘people first’ focus AND to get their everyone needs to upgrade his or her operating system to have a very high ability to leverage ‘essential human skills’.
Also the term ‘soft skills’ has often been labeled as feminine and this too has created a push back response by ‘driver’ type personalities.
Years ago workplaces could tolerate people who had certain technical skills without ‘essential human skills’ – today with the fast pace of change people need to be able to adapt and respond to a variety of situations.
Human awareness is an essential component of ‘essential human skills’ – the more aware we are on how to connect with people of diverse cultures, opinions, and values the better we can collaborate and innovate.
As technology continues to exponentially shift the way we live and work the focus will be on how we as humans are connecting, collaborating and solving the world’s problems.
Every time you hear someone say ‘soft skills’ please invite them to start saying ‘essential human skills’. Talk to your company about shifting the language.
In my book,“NextMapping – Anticipate, Navigate and Create The Future of Work”I share research on how it is the ‘human behavior’ trends shaping the pace of disruption. As technology simplifies and automates a lot of ‘hard’ tasks the imperative is for us as humans to uplevel our ‘essential human skills’.
immediately and effectively.The obvious question then becomes “how do we get others to be Change Leaders?” We are on the train, we know where we are going, we want to lead change, however there are people around us that perhaps are not thinking or acting the same way. How do we engage, motivate and inspire them to be change leaders?
It is different than everyday ‘normal’ leadership that we are used to!
