There is a new generation in town – its Generation Covid also known as Gen C. which includes young people under the age of twenty.
Traditionalists 1925 to 1945 (known for being the war generation)
There is no question that the social impact of the pandemic on everyone is yet to be completely known. It is however particularly interesting to watch and see how Gen C’s experience with the pandemic will shift their choices and behaviors for the future.
Needless to say the social change that Gen C will bring will be radical and disruptive to the status quo. There will be many changes created by Generation Covid and the future.
I have heard stories of elementary school aged kids having high levels of anxiety due to either being schooled only online or dealing with constant opens and closures of their schools.
Middle school and high school youngsters have either been doing their schooling predominantly online or a hybrid of part time in class and part time online.
Gen C will be known as the pandemic generation who will irrevocably change the future.
In order to start to look at the impact of a generation on the future we need to understand the cultural dynamics that include their feelings, their values, the environmental influences and their outlooks shaped by the events of their era.
However parents have also reported positive environmental influences of the pandemic and its impact on their children such as:
Increased empathy and awareness around other people
Given the range of complex feelings that Gen C have navigated AND given the positive environmental influences it could be safe to surmise the following predictions for Generation Covid and the future:
Gen C will see gender inequalities and inequities as outmoded and untenable and will seek to bring balance and equality for the future.
Gen C will be the generation where many of parents reverse migrated to rural living and this will results in Gen C having a deeper connection to the earth and therefore the environment.
Gen C will deeply understand the importance of mental well being and health and this will lead to a focus on increased solutions for youth and adults to improve overall health.
I don’t know about you but I am excited about the impact that Gen C will have on the future.
I hold high optimism that the disruptions Gen C are living through will equip them to help us all create a better future.
Being a leader pre-pandemic was still very traditional in that leaders had workers working primarily in office. When the pandemic happened it sped up the future of work reality of ‘remote work’.
The remote work genie is now out of the bottle. Which means we are never going back to the way work was done prior to the pandemic.
Greater ability to focus with less in office distractions
Of course the remote work reality has caused some leaders to be concerned as businesses prepare to re-open and to call workers back into the office.
The skills that leaders need today and for the future include:
Increased EQ (emotional intelligence) to lead with compassion and care
A ‘people first’ leadership focus where people are valued over profits
Leading hybrid teams has unique challenges such as:
For example the in office team can feel as if they are ‘working harder’ than remote teams. Conversely remote teams can feel as though they are working harder due to having blurred lines between work hours and personal time.
The keys to leaders being successful at leading hybrid teams include building new skills and reskilling to be a future ready leader. The biggest shift leaders need to make is to shift his or her mindset about what it means to be a leader.
Shared leadership does not mean too many cooks in the kitchen what it means is that workers are valued for their strengths. A leader leading hybrid teams has the opportunity to increase understanding of people in general.
Leaders who value people as people and who see leadership as an opportunity to grow and learn
Leaders who value diversity of culture, gender and personalities and truly commit to respecting the diversity of the teams
Leaders who leverage innovation by asking for ALL team members to share ideas consistently via polling/surveying/anecdotal feedback
Leaders who can hear feedback from their teams without defensiveness. In addition leaders take the feedback they have gathered and apply what has been learned right away.
Leaders who are sensitive to the unique challenges of workers working in the office as well as the unique challenges of workers working remotely.
Out of challenge comes opportunity – I think we will look back at the pandemic and the changes it forced as a positive thing. For leaders there is much change ahead and the ability to lead hybrid teams is going to be an in demand skill and leaders who can master leading hybrid teams will be guaranteed job stability for the future.
Although each of these industries may seem disparate and unalike the truth is that every single unique industry has commonalities with all industries.
The future workplace requires the defense industry to shift the narrative towards the good that they do in disaster relief. This requires a look at the trends of workers seeking to work in industries that are positively impacting the world.
Ultimately the single biggest change that is going to propel organizations into the next iteration of being future-ready is this:
When leaders and teams make this big change it will create the future of work and quickly.
Often after I deliver a keynote or facilitate a workshop on the future of work I have people ask :
The ‘how’ to make the big change of getting everyone’s mindset focused on future and solution includes:
Consistent focus and communication on the ‘future mindset’ that is the new cultural norm.
Reward and recognition for the ‘change leaders’ demonstrating creativity, agility, and innovation.
My response was this if leaders were to focus on one single strategic focus for the next year of helping to shift and support the mindsets of all workers there would be dramatic positive shifts in business overall.
In the past workers were not asked or required to think strategically – today and in the future it is imperative. A future-ready mindset requires a focus on future and opportunity, a willingness to look at the big picture, an ability to factor in other departments and other leaders, to move beyond silos and the ability for everyone to think like a leader.
