Most beer-loving travelers have Munich high on their to-do list.
No, not the crowds.
But the beer is double the strength!
Munich, Bavaria capital, is home to centuries-old buildings and numerous museums.
You’ll be happy to know that it is a very visitor-friendly city, in almost every aspect.
The country has the reputation of being unfriendly, but it’s far from the truth.
One of the possible elements of “culture shock” might be how even in the busiest downtown areas, it can still get very quiet on a weekday winter night or a Sunday; Munich can offer many pros of city life (great restaurants, beautiful buildings, plenty ofthings to do) minus a lot of hustle, bustle, and stress.
And it goes without saying that the beer is world-famous for a reason!
A LOCAL SECRET For around three weeks in early spring, breweries all across Munich set up their beer halls.
In fact, this beer festival might be the city’s best-kept secret.
The crafty monks concocted a beer beverage to sustain them through the Lent fast, and called their pick-me-up ‘blessed father’s beer’ or ‘the holy oil of St. Francis’.
And where exactly did the Munich Strong Beer Festival come from?
Forced to fast during Lent, monks began to brew starkbier as a loophole way to get their fill of calories.
To sum things up: starkbier is a strong beer, it’s stronger in alcohol content but also in dissolved solids, it will fill you up and mess you up.
Quick Tips for Munich Strong Beer Festival
