With this guide, we hope to help you know how to survive the first day in Rust and you can build your first base so that you can fully enjoy the game.
So many things are happening in Rust that you could explore the options until your character starves.
With such a relentless landscape, finding your way through your first session can be a real challenge. Before you start, take a look at this guide so you can how to survive the first day in Rust.
When you first appear, you will be homeless and naked, with only a stone and a torch.
Any tree will serve as wood, but you will have to be on the lookout for the correct type of stones to harvest. The goal is to work to build a starting base.
This will make harvesting much faster, so you can reduce the amount of time you are outside vulnerable. You must be on the lookout for hemp plants, which will provide you with the fabric you need for a hunting bow and the essential sleeping bag, which is the respawn point of Rust.
You should also be on the lookout for berries and mushrooms for the same reason, as staying fed is difficult.
However, most points of interest are PvP hot spots, so focus on gathering a pile of wood and stone first.
Once you have some full piles of wood and stone, you will have to find a place away from any other players and get a simple basic setup.
Then you can use the construction plan to place two square foundations connected to each other. Use your right click to change what you are building and place the square walls on all but one side, where you can place a door instead.
Right click with your hammer on the foundation, walls and ceiling and upgrade them to stone if you can. ´
Next, right-click on the lock and make sure to close the door. You do not need to create a key, the door will know that it is you since you placed it (the doors of Rust they operate with some kind of magic) and now you've at least locked the inside.
Your tool cabinet should be stocked with construction material, which you will use over time to keep the base standing. If you neglect it, your frame will start to lose health and eventually collapse.
These are all points of interest and they also litter the sides of the roads of Rust. They are tempting targets for everyone, as they are essentially loot piñatas, so be careful when farming them.
They are always worth the detour, just watch out for other players.
Players are the deadliest obstacle in Rust, and the worst place to deal with them is right outside your own base.
You can easily do this by attaching a triangular base to the existing one with the door and then building a new door from it. It's also a good idea to drop a few spikes or other nuisances on your roof if you can, just to convince anyone to stay away.
